## 🔍 Security Audit: `app.js`

### Vulnerability Summary

| # | OWASP Category | Severity | Finding |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | A01 – Broken Access Control | 🔴 Critical | `/users` endpoint exposes all user data with zero authentication or authorization |
| 2 | A02 – Cryptographic Failures | 🟠 High | No HTTPS enforcement; data transmitted in plaintext |
| 3 | A03 – Injection | 🟠 High | No input validation or sanitization on any request input |
| 4 | A05 – Security Misconfiguration | 🟠 High | No security headers (e.g., `helmet`); Express version fingerprinting enabled |
| 5 | A07 – Identification & Auth Failures | 🔴 Critical | No authentication middleware on any route |
| 6 | A08 – Software & Data Integrity | 🟡 Medium | No `package.json` / lockfile — dependency integrity unverifiable |
| 7 | A09 – Security Logging & Monitoring | 🟡 Medium | No request logging, error logging, or anomaly detection |
| 8 | A04 – Insecure Design | 🟡 Medium | No rate limiting — open to brute-force and DoS attacks |
| 9 | Sensitive Data Exposure | 🟠 High | User PII (emails) returned with no masking, access control, or field filtering |
| 10 | Error Handling | 🟡 Medium | No global error handler — stack traces could leak to clients |

---

### Detailed Findings & Fixes

---

#### 🔴 1. Broken Access Control — No Authentication on `/users`
**Risk:** Any anonymous request can enumerate all users and harvest PII (names, emails).

```js
// ❌ VULNERABLE — completely open endpoint
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([{ id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' }]);
});
```
```js
// ✅ FIX — protect with auth middleware
const authenticate = (req, res, next) => {
  const token = req.headers['authorization']?.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
  try {
    req.user = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    next();
  } catch {
    res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
  }
};

app.get('/users', authenticate, (req, res) => { ... });
```

---

#### 🟠 2. Security Misconfiguration — No Security Headers
**Risk:** Without headers like `X-Frame-Options`, `Content-Security-Policy`, etc., the app is vulnerable to clickjacking, MIME sniffing, and XSS. Express also advertises itself via the `X-Powered-By` header.

```js
// ❌ VULNERABLE — no headers set, fingerprinting enabled
const app = express();
```
```js
// ✅ FIX — add helmet and disable fingerprinting
const helmet = require('helmet');
app.use(helmet());
app.disable('x-powered-by'); // also done by helmet, but explicit is better
```

---

#### 🔴 3. No Input Validation or Sanitization
**Risk:** Any route that later accepts query params or body data (a near-certainty as the app grows) is immediately vulnerable to injection attacks — SQL injection, NoSQL injection, XSS, etc.

```js
// ❌ VULNERABLE — no body parsing or validation configured
const app = express();
```
```js
// ✅ FIX — add body parsing with size limits, and validation
const express = require('express');
app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' })); // prevent large payload DoS
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false, limit: '10kb' }));

// For route params, use a validator (example with express-validator)
