## Finding 3 — Sensitive Data Exposure on `/users`
**OWASP A02: Cryptographic Failures / A01: Broken Access Control | Severity: HIGH**

The `/users` endpoint returns user data (including email addresses) with **no authentication check** whatsoever. Any unauthenticated caller can enumerate all users and their PII. In a real application this endpoint would also typically expose far more sensitive fields.

**Fix — require authentication and strip sensitive fields:**
```js
// Middleware to verify a JWT (npm install jsonwebtoken)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const authHeader = req.headers['authorization'];
  const token = authHeader && authHeader.split(' ')[1]; // Bearer <token>

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication required.' });
  }

  jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET, (err, user) => {
    if (err) return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token.' });
    req.user = user;
    next();
  });
}

// Apply auth guard and return only non-sensitive fields
app.get('/users', requireAuth, (req, res) => {
  const users = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice' }, // ✅ email omitted unless the caller needs it
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob'   },
  ];
  res.json(users);
});
```

---

## Finding 4 — No Request Body Parsing or Input Validation
**OWASP A03: Injection | Severity: HIGH**

There is no body parser and no input validation on any route. If POST/PUT routes are added later (almost certain), raw unvalidated user input will flow directly into the application — a textbook path to SQL injection, NoSQL injection, and XSS.

**Fix — add body parsing and schema validation with `zod`:**
```js
// npm install zod
const { z } = require('zod');

app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' })); // Body parser with size cap
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false, limit: '10kb' }));

// Example: validated POST /users route
const CreateUserSchema = z.object({
  name:  z.string().min(1).max(100).trim(),
  email: z.string().email().max(254),
});

app.post('/users', requireAuth, (req, res) => {
  const result = CreateUserSchema.safeParse(req.body);
  if (!result.success) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid input.', details: result.error.flatten() });
  }

  const { name, email } = result.data; // ✅ Safe, validated data only
  // ... proceed to DB
  res.status(201).json({ message: 'User created.' });
});
```

